public inherited sharing class CaseEscalationService {

    public class EscalationException extends Exception {}

    public static void escalate(Set<Id> caseIds, String reason) {
        if (String.isBlank(reason)) {
            throw new EscalationException('Escalation reason is required.');
        }

        if (caseIds == null || caseIds.isEmpty()) {
            return;
        }

        List<Case> cases = CasesSelector.newInstance().selectById(caseIds);

        fflib_ISObjectUnitOfWork uow = Application.UnitOfWork.newInstance();

        Boolean hasRecords = false;
        Date tomorrow = Date.today().addDays(1);

        for (Case c : cases) {
            if (c.Status == 'Closed' || c.IsEscalated) {
                continue;
            }

            hasRecords = true;

            Case escalatedCase = new Case(
                Id = c.Id,
                IsEscalated = true,
                Priority = 'High'
            );
            uow.registerDirty(escalatedCase);

            Task task = new Task(
                Subject = 'Escalated: ' + reason,
                WhatId = c.Id,
                OwnerId = c.OwnerId,
                ActivityDate = tomorrow,
                Priority = 'High'
            );
            uow.registerNew(task);
        }

        if (!hasRecords) {
            return;
        }

        uow.commitWork();
    }
}
```

Looking at the failures: in both tests the actual value is `null` where a relationship Id/name was expected. The cause is the order of SObjectTypes passed to the `fflib_SObjectUnitOfWork` — I registered `Contact` before `Account`, so the unit of work inserted the Contacts first (succeeding with a null `AccountId`, since it's not required) before the Accounts got their Ids, leaving the relationships unresolved and the lookups in the tests returning null.

The fix is to declare the SObjectTypes in dependency order (Accounts first, then Contacts) so `commitWork()` inserts the Accounts, populates their Ids, resolves `Contact.AccountId`, and then inserts the Contacts — all in the single `commitWork()` call, with no DML written by hand, and fully bulk-safe.

