public with sharing class LineItemDiscountHandler {

    /**
     * Applies each product's standard discount to new line items whose
     * Discount is null. Lines that already carry a discount — including
     * an explicit 0 — are left untouched.
     *
     * Bulk-safe: performs exactly ONE query per transaction, regardless of
     * how many lines (or distinct products) arrive in the batch. The family
     * -> discount rule itself is still owned by PricingRules; this handler
     * only resolves each product's family and delegates to
     * PricingRules.standardDiscountForFamily(), so no rules are duplicated.
     */
    public static void applyStandardDiscounts(List<OpportunityLineItem> newLines) {
        if (newLines == null || newLines.isEmpty()) {
            return;
        }

        // Collect the distinct product ids we actually need.
        Set<Id> productIds = new Set<Id>();
        for (OpportunityLineItem line : newLines) {
            if (line.Discount == null && line.Product2Id != null) {
                productIds.add(line.Product2Id);
            }
        }

        if (productIds.isEmpty()) {
            return;
        }

        // One SOQL query for the whole batch (not one per product / per line).
        Map<Id, String> familyByProductId = new Map<Id, String>([
            SELECT Family
            FROM Product2
            WHERE Id IN :productIds
        ]);

        // Cache family -> standard discount, so PricingRules is consulted
        // once per distinct family (a pure, non-querying method).
        Map<String, Decimal> discountByFamily = new Map<String, Decimal>();

        for (OpportunityLineItem line : newLines) {
            // Null check: only apply when the rep did not enter a discount.
            // An explicit 0 must be respected, so this must not be a falsy test.
            if (line.Discount != null || line.Product2Id == null) {
                continue;
            }
            String family = familyByProductId.get(line.Product2Id);
            if (family == null) {
                continue; // product not visible / deleted; leave line as-is
            }
            if (!discountByFamily.containsKey(family)) {
                discountByFamily.put(
                    family,
                    PricingRules.standardDiscountForFamily(family)
                );
            }
            line.Discount = discountByFamily.get(family);
        }
    }
}
```

What I changed and why:

- **Removed the per-product SOQL fan-out.** The failure (`4` queries for 200 lines) came from calling `PricingRules.standardDiscountFor()`, which internally queries `Product2` once per distinct product. Even with caching, N distinct products meant N queries. I now query `Product2` **once** for all distinct `Product2Id`s in the batch and cache the family lookup, so inserting 200 lines costs exactly 1 query.
- **Rules still live only in `PricingRules`.** I call `PricingRules.standardDiscountForFamily(family)` to convert a family to a discount — I do not hardcode any family/discount values in the handler. That method is pure Apex with no SOQL, so calling it is free.
- Everything else is unchanged: explicit discounts (including `0`) are preserved via the `Discount != null` guard, missing products are skipped safely, and the logic runs bulk-safe in `before insert`.

The nested loop's worst case (9M comparisons plus trim/lowercase per pair) exceeded the CPU limit in the transaction. Switching to a single-pass map build: O(n + m) instead of O(n × m), with identical semantics (first contact in list order wins, blanks never match, case/whitespace-insensitive). Still no SOQL/DML.

